[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609071200-base-account-default-and-agent-registration"
 :adr/doc_type "adr"
 :adr/topic "base-account-default-and-agent-registration"
 :adr/title "Base Account is the default account on kotobase.net and kotoba.cloud, and agents register themselves from a key"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-07"
 :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Fable 5.1"]
 :adr/related ["2609070400" "net-kotobase/control-plane ADR-2609071000" "net-kotobase/control-plane ADR-2609021400"]
 :adr/repos ["com-junkawasaki/root" "net-kotobase/control-plane" "kotoba-lang/app-kotoba-cloud"]
 :adr/validation {:agent-smoke "npm run test:agent-account 20/20 against Base mainnet" :live "npm run test:agent-account:live 21/21, test:smart-account:live 20/20, test:phrase:live 12/12 on kotobase-authn 890da019" :gate "root-human-auth-no-downgrade exit 0 on this tree" :scope "the deployed ceremony and the policy inventory; a human keys.coinbase.com ceremony in a browser is not measured"}
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nOwner instruction (2026-09-07): https://kotobase.net/ and https://kotoba.cloud/ must let a visitor create an account with a Base smart-contract wallet by default, and an AI agent or bot must be able to create an account immediately by its own operation.\n\nMeasured before the change: both apexes handed off to auth.kotoba.cloud/sign-in (net-kotobase/control-plane, `authn/`), whose first section was the passkey and whose wallet section required an injected `window.ethereum`. A Base Account (Coinbase Smart Wallet, an ERC-4337 account owned by a passkey made at keys.coinbase.com) is not an injected provider, so a visitor without a browser extension could not use one at all. The server already verified contract-account signatures on Base mainnet (ERC-1271 / ERC-6492, control-plane ADR-2609021400, live since 2026-09-02); the gap was the client, plus the absence of any agent-facing route. Agents could authenticate with a did:key CACAO and received a browser cookie and an account of the only kind that existed, `human`.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. auth.kotoba.cloud/sign-in leads with a Base Account section and primary action. The Base Account SDK (`@base-org/account` 2.5.10, Apache-2.0) is vendored into the authn Worker and served from the same origin at `/vendor/base-account.js`, so the page keeps `script-src 'self'`; `connect-src` admits keys.coinbase.com and Coinbase's two RPC proxies and not its analytics host (telemetry off). The wallet writes and signs the EIP-4361 message (ERC-7846 `wallet_connect` + `signInWithEthereum`) and the existing `POST /v1/siwe/verify` asks Base mainnet. kotoba.cloud's primary action reads `Start with a Base Account`; kotobase.net's `/signup` already hands off to the page.\n\n2. `POST /v1/agents/register {message, signature}`: the same `wallet_connect` ceremony, declared as an agent. By default the agent's secp256k1 key is the owner (`abi.encode(address)`, 32 bytes) of a counterfactual Coinbase Smart Wallet, `factory.getAddress([owner], 0)`, and that account signs the message's replay-safe hash, wrapped as `SignatureWrapper{0, sig}` inside an ERC-6492 deployable signature; Base mainnet verifies it before any deployment. The principal is the account's `did:pkh:eip155:8453:<account>`, so an agent's account is a Base smart-contract wallet by default, exactly like a person's. `--eoa` signs as the bare key. A first sign-in creates an account of kind `agent` (`authn.account/principal-kinds #{human agent}`), reported as `principalKind` on `/v1/session` and `user.kind` on `/v1/identities`; the request's cookie is ignored so the route can never link into or step up a human account. Client half `authn.agent-account` (nbb), CLI `npm run agent:account -- keygen | address | register`.\n\n3. Policy: no rule in `manifest/human-authentication-policy.edn` v2 changes. The inventory entries for `authn` and `app-kotoba-cloud` now declare the three approved methods with `:siwe-erc1271` as the default, remain `:unverified` (the legacy-closure negative suite was not re-run today), and carry today's live evidence. Root SECURITY.md names the agent registration route inside the agent boundary paragraph.\n\n## The gap, and how it closed (2026-09-08)\n\nThis ADR shipped with the nested `principal.kind` on the wire still saying `human` for agent sessions, because the kotobase.net gateway (`kotobase.core/verified-principal`) validated that closed projection against `#{human service-account}` and an unknown kind would have invalidated the whole viewer. The follow-up was to teach the gateway `agent`, deploy it first, then switch the projection.\n\nThat was done the next day, and doing it surfaced a larger live defect first: the gateway had never admitted `:siwe` as an authentication method at all, so EVERY wallet session — the Base Account this ADR made the default, the recovery phrase, and every agent registered here — resolved to an anonymous viewer at kotobase.net while authn reported it valid. Measured with one cookie: `GET auth.kotobase.net/v1/session` valid with `authnMethods [siwe]`, `GET kotobase.net/api/auth/me` 401; the same route with a CACAO session, 200. Both services' suites were green throughout, each being right about itself.\n\nnet-kotobase/control-plane ADR-2609080200 records the fix in two ordered deploys — the gateway (Worker 4a24f371) admitting `:siwe` and learning `:agent`, then authn (Worker 3c253e53) emitting the nested kind — plus the cross-service suite `npm run test:gateway-session` that measures the seam from the outside with controls on both sides. It was 2-red/6-green against production before the fix and is 9/9 after, with the gateway now reporting `principalType: agent` for a wallet session.\n\nStill open: the did:key CACAO agent path (`/v1/cacao/session`) creates `human` accounts. An agent that authenticates that way is indistinguishable from a person in the account record.\n\n## What was measured (2026-09-07)\n\n- `npm run test:agent-account` (nbb, no JVM) against the real CoinbaseSmartWalletFactory and account code on Base mainnet: fresh key, 32-byte owner, deterministic `getAddress`, replay-safe hash, ECDSA, SignatureWrapper, ERC-6492 wrap; the validator answers 0x01 for the real signature and 0x00 for the same signature over another digest and for another key signing for the same account. 20/20.\n- authn `clojure -M:test` 58 tests / 371 assertions; `npm run test:siwe` 8/8; Worker bundle 1,849,216 bytes (493,458 gzip), the increase being the vendored SDK.\n- Deployed `kotobase-authn` version 890da019-8e98-49af-9ca7-e2500867bdea. Live: `npm run test:agent-account:live` 21/21 (agent registers as a key-owned Base Account, `did:pkh:eip155:8453:<account>`, `principalKind agent`, `signer contract`, session cookie; replay 401; unissued nonce 401; missing signature 400; `--eoa` lands on the key's did:pkh; the same key re-enters the same account; `/vendor/base-account.js` served; the Base Account section is first on the page; CSP as designed). Regressions `test:smart-account:live` 20/20 and `test:phrase:live` 12/12.\n- kotoba-lang/app-kotoba-cloud: `npm test` 51 tests / 492 assertions (after `npm run build:contract`), worker smoke passed, deployed `kotoba-cloud-control-plane` version 9c440df8-0750-46c8-a266-1f1f7ba6e410; live `/` and `/ja/` carry `Start with a Base Account` / `Base Account で始める`.\n- Measured in a real browser on 2026-09-08 (ADR-2609080100, net-kotobase/control-plane): `npm run test:browser-passkey` drives the deployed sign-in page in Chrome against a CDP virtual authenticator, 23/23 — registration, sign-in, the Base Account the passkey owns (`did:pkh:eip155:8453:0xff2b4a...`, validated by Base mainnet), and a refused user verification with the control that shows the refusal is attributable to UV. Still not measured: the keys.coinbase.com popup, because completing it creates an account on a third party's production service and headless Chrome does not reliably open it; what it would exercise on our side is the same contract-account signature the passkey Base Account and the agent e2e already verify first-party.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Both products can create a Base smart-contract wallet account for a visitor with no extension; Kotoba never sees a key.\n- Agents get the same kind of account in one round trip after the nonce, from any language: the bytes are pinned by the smoke against the chain.\n- Two principal kinds exist; policy that should differ for agents can read `principalKind`, and the nested-projection gap is named, not hidden.\n- The agent CLI is nbb because it needs secp256k1, Keccak and ABI encoding that kbb's capability set does not carry; it is a kbb migration candidate.\n"}]
